42c12dc9f7.en:
- ~~each slice was a dead end~~ — **resolved on 2026-08-27**: navigation and position in the queue, generated in the same order as the gate collects;
- ~~labels living only in the table~~ — **resolved on 2026-08-27** in the `o-circulo`, the only case: cosine, sine, and arc passed to have their names attached to the stroke;
- ~~the page jumped between slices~~ — **resolved on 2026-08-27**: a common height of 568 px on the screen for the nine, with each composition centered in its own frame and untouched;
- **the fusion.** The nine are slices by design — small and sharp, to blend well afterward. The first overlap examined was *the wave* of the `o-circulo` × the `desenrolamento`. I cut the wave because it touched the ground, and the **author reverted** — rightly, for a reason my analysis did not reach: the wave is a **methodological tool**, it forms the mental image of the phenomenon, and seeing the curve *being drawn* enriches understanding. Structural coherence is not the only rule for a didactic sheet. Upon restoration, what the cut had hidden appeared: **the old version never drew the curve progressively** — it painted the entire sine wave at once, and the `rastro` was declared and never used, with the prose asserting a behavior that the code did not have. Now the curve is traced from −6.2 to the current angle: animating draws, dragging the angle makes the stroke advance and retreat. Both sheets remain, and the difference is stated in both: here the **image** of the phenomenon, with the axis compressed and declared; in `desenrolamento`, the same passage **at scale**, with arc and tangent. The next contacts to examine are the triangle drawn in the `o-circulo` × `o-triangulo`, and the tangent line, which appears in three sheets;
- ~~the point T goes out of frame~~ — **resolved on 2026-08-27**, and the fix became content. The old cut was `|r·tg| < 2.6`: the simple tangent **disappeared** when it became large, which is exactly when it is doing what the sheet wants to show. A sweep of 73,200 combinations measured T out of frame in **23% of them** — all with the tangent disappearing silently. Now it is cut **at the edge**, with an arrowhead and the value displayed: the cut stopped hiding the asymptote and started showing it. The sweep confirms 0 points out of frame after the cut;
- ~~the branch of order~~ — **resolved on 2026-08-27.** The rate dropped from second to seventh and the three declared debts of it zeroed out. What remains is the distinction, now written: dependence has two branches, reading is a queue;
- ~~the sheet occupied the entire monitor~~ — **resolved on 2026-08-27**, and the correction came from the author with the screen in hand. On a monitor of 1900 px the drawing opened with **1848 × 954** and the title and text turned into a caption of a giant figure; the text at the top stated the thesis and stopped, without saying what to do with the hands. Now prose is in charge: a single measure (`--col`, 620 px, about 80 characters per line) for the title and all text blocks — different widths with automatic margin would give misaligned left edges —, text **justified and centered**, and the drawing in a frame of **1100 × 568**. Each sheet gained a **Do** block, which names one by one the controls that exist in it;
- ~~the frame of the drawing was the same in the nine~~ — **resolved on 2026-08-27.** It was 1240 × 640 in all, and for a composition almost square that left hundreds of pixels of empty space on both sides. Now **each slice has the proportion of its own drawing**, and the number is not a guess: a browser sweeps all controls of each sheet and measures what the composition actually occupies; the frame is this rectangle with 28 px of leeway. The emptiness fell from 43% to 15% in `par-vira-ponto`, and from 38% to 16% in `o-triangulo`. On the screen the **height is fixed** (568 px) and it is the width that tells the shape — from 658 px in the most square to 1100 px in the graph ones —, and the controls are aligned with the frame, not with the page. The measurement also corrected two of my assumptions: the `o-circulo` *seemed* small and in fact uses 1226 of the 1240 px; and the five wide slices fill the frame **by construction**, because they draw magnitudes that go to infinity and are purposefully cut at the edge;
- the `conferir_layout.py` measures all this in a real browser — column, centering, proportion, controls, and overflow — and has negative control: undoing the width of the page reproduces the 954 px, and letting the controls out of the frame disapproves. It never returns "ok" for lack of proof;
- **human visual QA.** The two gates measure what is mechanical. No slice was judged by human eye against an aesthetic ruler, and that does not automate;
- **no learning test.** It was not measured if someone learns more from this. The assertion of this repository is about what the figures *show*, not about an effect measured in a student.

